NAME
       shred - overwrite a file to make its contents hard to recover

SYNOPSIS
       shred [-u] [-n N] [-z] [-v] FILE...

DESCRIPTION
       shred writes random data over a file's contents several times, so the
       old data cannot be read back with recovery tools. The file itself
       stays (with the random contents) unless you add -u.

       On modern file systems and SSDs (journaling, copy-on-write, wear
       levelling) old copies of the data may survive elsewhere on the disk,
       so shred gives no guarantee there.

OPTIONS
       -u, --remove
              Delete the file after overwriting it.

       -n, --iterations N
              Overwrite N times (default 3).

       -z, --zero
              Finish with a pass of zeros, to hide the shredding.

       -v, --verbose
              Show progress.

EXAMPLES
           shred -u -v /root/old-secrets.txt

SEE ALSO
       rm(1), dd(1)
